1.4  Interrupts: how devices get attention

TL;DR
  - A device that needs the CPU (a packet arrived, a disk finished, a key was
    pressed) raises an interrupt: the CPU stops what it's doing and runs the
    kernel's handler for that device.
  - The handler does only the urgent part; the rest runs a moment later, as a
    software interrupt (softirq) or in a kernel thread.
  - The timer interrupt is the kernel's heartbeat: it's how the scheduler gets
    the CPU back.

      cat /proc/interrupts
      watch -d -n1 cat /proc/softirqs      # -d highlights what changes

On your machine
  Hardware  LOC Local timer interrupts 900, iwlwifi 300, RES Rescheduling
            interrupts 170, nvme0q0 70, timer 10, NMI Non-maskable interrupts 2
  Software  TIMER 70, NET_RX 45, SCHED 40
  CPUs      2 share the work

Pitfalls
  - The counters only grow from boot: compare two readings, or watch them.
  - One CPU taking every interrupt of a busy network card can become the
    bottleneck. Multi-queue cards and irqbalance spread them over CPUs.
  - ksoftirqd using CPU means deferred work piled up, often under heavy network
    traffic.

Deep dive: 2 questions. Read them with idk 1.4 --why

Related
  Commands  watch
  Paths     /proc/interrupts, /proc/softirqs
